-- title:
--   Polynomial clearing of a pulled-back section on an edge region
-- statement:
--   The statement is set up with a Čerednik–Drinfeld quotient datum, an analytic frame, and three auxiliary hypotheses, and concludes that an interpolating function on Drinfeld's upper half plane becomes rigid-holomorphic on each edge region of an affine chart after multiplication by a nonzero polynomial.
--
--   **Arithmetic data.** A prime $r$; a characteristic-zero domain $\mathcal O$ which is a discrete valuation ring (`hdvr`), an element $\pi$ with `hπ : Irreducible π`, with $\mathcal O$ complete for the $\pi$-adic filtration (`hcomplete`), residue cardinality $\#(\mathcal O/\pi)=r$ (`hres`) and $(r)=(\pi)$ as ideals of $\mathcal O$ (`hunr`); a characteristic-zero fraction field $K_0$ of $\mathcal O$.
--
--   **Unramified coefficients.** A characteristic-zero domain $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ over $\mathcal O$ together with an $\mathcal O$-algebra automorphism $\mathrm{Fr}$, subject to: $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ is complete for the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$ (`hOnr_complete`), that ideal is maximal (`hOnr_max`), every element of $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ satisfies a monic polynomial over $\mathcal O$ modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_alg`), every monic polynomial of positive degree over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ has a root modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_closed`), and $\mathrm{Fr}(x)\equiv x^{r}\pmod\pi$ for all $x$ (`hFr`).
--
--   **Determinant valuation.** A homomorphism $v\!\det : \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)\to\mathbb Z$ (written multiplicatively) characterised by `hvdet`: $v\!\det(g)=n$ if and only if $\det g = u\pi^{n}$ for some unit $u$ of $\mathcal O$.
--
--   **Group data.** A group $G$, a homomorphism $\sigma : G\to\mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ and a subgroup $\Gamma$ such that $\Gamma$ contains an element acting by a scalar matrix and with $v\!\det = 2$ (`hcent`) and an element with $v\!\det = 1$ (`hodd`); a subgroup $\Gamma'$ which, by `hΓ'`, consists exactly of the elements of $\Gamma$ with even $v\!\det\circ\sigma$. Further, $\rho : G\to \mathrm{PGL}_2(K_0)$ is the projectivisation of $\sigma$ (`hρ`), and the image $\rho(\Gamma')$ acts on the vertices of the Bruhat–Tits tree $\mathrm{Vertex}\,\mathcal O\,K_0$ (homothety classes of full lattices) with finite vertex stabilisers (`hdisc`) and with a finite set $S$ of vertices meeting every orbit (`hcocpt`).
--
--   **The quotient scheme and its moduli description.** A scheme $\mathcal X$ with a proper flat morphism $f : \mathcal X\to\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$, and a family $\Theta$ assigning, to every $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ in which $\pi$ is nilpotent, a map from $(O^{\mathrm{nr}}\to_{\mathcal O} B)\times \mathrm{DeligneDatum}(\pi,B)$ (the product `AlgFunctor.prod (AlgFunctor.corep Onr) (Omega K₀ π)`) to $\{\varphi : \operatorname{Spec} B\to\mathcal X \mid \varphi \text{ followed by } f = \operatorname{Spec}\text{-structure map}\}$ (`Scheme.nilpPoints f`). The hypotheses on $\Theta$ are: naturality in $B$ (`hΘnat`); invariance under the twisted $\Gamma$-action, i.e. $\Theta\,x'=\Theta\,x$ whenever `OmegaNr.IsTwistedAct π Onr Fr vdet B (σ γ) x x'` holds for some $\gamma\in\Gamma$, that predicate asserting that the $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$-component of $x'$ is the $\mathrm{Fr}^{-v\!\det(\sigma\gamma)}$-twist of that of $x$ and that the Deligne datum of $x'$ is the pullback of that of $x$ along $(\sigma\gamma)^{-1}$ (`hΘinv`); bijectivity on fibres over algebraically closed fields: for each algebraically closed $\mathcal O$-field $k$ with $\pi$ nilpotent and each $\psi : O^{\mathrm{nr}}\to_{\mathcal O}k$, every point of `(Scheme.nilpPoints f).obj k` is of the form $\Theta(\psi,P)$, and $\Theta(\psi,P)=\Theta(\psi,P')$ holds precisely when $(\psi,P)$ and $(\psi,P')$ are related by the twisted action of some $\gamma\in\Gamma$ (`hΘfib`); and universality: for every scheme $T$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ and every natural, twist-invariant family $\rho'$ of $T$-valued points there is a natural transformation $u$ from `Scheme.nilpPoints f` to `Scheme.nilpPoints t` with $u\circ\Theta=\rho'$, and any natural $u'$ with $u'\circ\Theta=\rho'$ agrees with $u$ everywhere (`hΘuniv`).
--
--   **Analytic frame.** A field $C$ over $K_0$, valued in a linearly ordered commutative group with zero $\Gamma_0$, complete and algebraically closed; a commutative ring $R$ which is an $\mathcal O$-algebra and a subring-like source of scalars for $C$ (algebra maps $R\to C$, $\mathcal O\to C$ and the two scalar towers); a pseudo-uniformiser $\varpi$, that is an element $\varpi.\varpi\in K_0$ whose image in $C$ has valuation strictly between $0$ and $1$ and such that every nonzero $a\in K_0$ satisfies $v(\varpi)^N\le v(a)\le v(\varpi)^{-N}$ for some $N$; and `hF : IsAdicFrame π ϖ R`, which asserts that $\pi$ is irreducible, $R\to C$ is injective with image the closed unit ball of $C$, $R$ is $\pi$-adically complete, the elements of $K_0$ of valuation $\le 1$ are exactly those coming from $\mathcal O$, the image of $K_0$ is closed in $C$, and $\pi$ and $\varpi.\varpi$ have the same image in $C$. Moreover: an $\mathcal O$-algebra map $\psi_0 : O^{\mathrm{nr}}\to R$; the rank-one condition `hrk` that for $v(x)<1$ and $y\neq 0$ some power $v(x)^n$ is $\le v(y)$; the cofinality condition `hval` that every nonzero $\varepsilon\in\Gamma_0$ dominates $v(y)$ for some $y\neq 0$; `hex : Omega.IsExhausted ϖ`, that every point of the upper half plane $\Omega_C = C\setminus K_0$ lies in some affinoid $\mathrm{aff}_n = \{z : v(z)\le v(\varpi)^{-n} \text{ and } v(z-a)\ge v(\varpi)^{n} \text{ for all } a\in K_0 \text{ with } v(a)\le v(\varpi)^{-n}\}$; and the assumption that the ring `Omega.HolRingOf ϖ ρ` of functions on $\Omega_C$ holomorphic on every affinoid is a domain.
--
--   **Uniformisation of points.** A map $\Phi$ from `AdicPoint K₀ π R` — compatible systems of Deligne data over the quotients $R/(\pi^{n+1})$ — to the $R$-points of $f$, together with `hΦ`, a conjunction of four assertions: (i) for each $x$ and $n$, the reduction of $\Phi(x)$ to $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $R/(\pi^{n+1})$ is the point $\Theta$ attached to the pair consisting of the reduction of $\psi_0$ and $x.\mathrm{pt}\,n$; (ii) $\Phi$ is surjective; (iii) $\Phi(x)=\Phi(x')$ if and only if $x' = x\cdot\sigma\gamma$ for some $\gamma\in\Gamma'$; (iv) base change along $R\to C$ is injective on $R$-points of $f$, and every $C$-point of $f$ arises from an $R$-point by base change.
--
--   **Base point and the generic fibre.** A morphism $s_C : \operatorname{Spec} C\to\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ equal to $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the composite $\mathcal O\to K_0\to C$ (`hsC`), with the pullback $\mathcal X_C = \mathcal X\times_{\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O}\operatorname{Spec} C$ assumed integral.
--
--   **Hypothesis (A$'$), `hcover` (uniform algebraic clearing over a finite cover).** For every open $U$ of $\mathcal X_C$ containing its generic point and every section $s\in\mathcal O_{\mathcal X_C}(U)$ there are $\ell\in\mathbb N$ and opens $V_0,\dots,V_{\ell-1}$ of $\mathcal X$ such that every adic point $x$ satisfies $\top\le \Phi(x)^{-1}(V_a)$ for some $a$, and such that for each $a$ there are an open $W$ of $\mathcal X_C$ with $W\le \mathrm{pr}_1^{-1}(V_a)$ and containing the generic point, and finite families $\mathrm{num}_i\in\mathcal O_{\mathcal X}(V_a)$, $c_i\in C$ and $\mathrm{den}_j\in\mathcal O_{\mathcal X}(V_a)$, $c'_j\in C$, for which the section $D=\sum_j c'_j\,\mathrm{pr}_1^{*}\mathrm{den}_j$ is nonzero on $W$, and on $W\sqcap U$ one has $s\cdot D = \sum_i c_i\,\mathrm{pr}_1^{*}\mathrm{num}_i$ (the scalars being inserted through the second projection and the canonical identification of global sections of $\operatorname{Spec} C$ with $C$).
--
--   **Hypothesis (B), `htube` (tubewise holomorphy of pulled-back sections).** For every $g\in\mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$, every open $V$ of $\mathcal X$ and every $p\in\mathcal O_{\mathcal X}(V)$ there exist $m\in\mathbb N$ and finite sets $L_k, M_k\subseteq C\times C$ ($k\in\mathrm{Fin}\,m$) all of whose second coordinates are nonzero, such that, writing $E(g) = \mathrm{vertexTube}(\bar g)\cup\mathrm{edgeTube}(\bar g)\cup\mathrm{vertexTube}(\overline{g\cdot\mathrm{edgeFlip}})$ for the edge region (here $\bar g$ denotes the class of $g$ in $\mathrm{PGL}_2(K_0)$, $\mathrm{edgeFlip}$ is the diagonal matrix $\mathrm{diag}(\varpi.\varpi,1)$, $\mathrm{vertexTube}(h)$ is the set of $z\in\Omega_C$ with $h^{-1}z\in\mathrm{aff}_0$, and $\mathrm{edgeTube}(h)$ the set of $z\in\Omega_C$ with $h^{-1}z$ in the standard edge tube $v(\varpi)<v(\cdot)<1$), and writing $P_k$ for the piece of $E(g)$ cut out by the inequalities $v(e_2)\le v(\bar g^{-1}z-e_1)$ for $(e_1,e_2)\in L_k$ and $v(\bar g^{-1}z-e_1)\le v(e_2)$ for $(e_1,e_2)\in M_k$ (Möbius action via `Omega.pmoebius`): first, every adic point $x$ whose coordinate $z=x.\mathrm{toOmega}\,C$ lies in $E(g)$ and whose image $\Phi(x)$ factors through $V$ has $z\in P_k$ for some $k$; second, conversely, for each $k$, any adic point whose coordinate lies in $P_k$ has $\Phi(x)$ factoring through $V$; third, for each $k$ there is a function $F_k : P_k\to C$ lying in `Omega.holOn C P_k` — that is, a uniform limit on $P_k$ of a uniformly bounded sequence of rational functions with no pole on $P_k$ — such that $F_k(z)$ is the image in $C$ of the value of $p$ at $\Phi(x)$ whenever $z=x.\mathrm{toOmega}\,C$ and $\Phi(x)$ factors through $V$.
--
--   **Hypothesis (C), `hfin` (affinoid finiteness of exceptional coordinates).** For every open $U$ of $\mathcal X_C$ containing the generic point and every $n$, the set of points $z$ of the affinoid $\mathrm{aff}_n$ which are coordinates $z = x.\mathrm{toOmega}\,C$ of some adic point $x$ admitting a $C$-point $q$ of $\mathcal X_C$ over $\Phi(x)$ (that is, $q$ followed by $\mathrm{pr}_1$ equals the base change of $\Phi(x)$ along $R\to C$, and $q$ followed by $\mathrm{pr}_2$ is the identity) that does not factor through $U$, is finite.
--
--   **Conclusion.** Under these hypotheses the following holds. Let $U$ be an open of $\mathcal X_C$ containing its generic point, let $s\in\mathcal O_{\mathcal X_C}(U)$, and let $F : \Omega_C\to C$ be a function which interpolates $s$ in the sense that for every adic point $x$ and every $z\in\Omega_C$ with $z=x.\mathrm{toOmega}\,C$, and every $C$-point $q$ of $\mathcal X_C$ over $\Phi(x)$ as above which factors through $U$, the value $F(z)$ is the value of $s$ at $q$ (via the restriction to $\top$ and the identification of global sections of $\operatorname{Spec} C$ with $C$). Then for every $c\in K_0$, every $m\in\mathbb Z$, every $g\in\mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ whose matrix is $\begin{pmatrix}\varpi^{m-1} & c\\ 0 & 1\end{pmatrix}$, and every $n$ such that the edge region $E(g)$ is contained in the affinoid $\mathrm{aff}_n$, there exists a nonzero polynomial $b\in C[X]$ such that the function $z\mapsto b(z)\,F(z)$ on $E(g)$ belongs to `Omega.holOn C (E(g))`, the restriction of $F$ to $E(g)$ being taken through the inclusion of $\mathrm{aff}_n$ into $\Omega_C$.
--
--   This is the per-region step in the proof that a rational function on the Čerednik–Drinfeld quotient pulls back to a chartwise meromorphic function on Drinfeld's upper half plane: on the closed edge region of an affine chart $\begin{pmatrix}\varpi^{m-1} & c\\ 0&1\end{pmatrix}$, multiplication by a single nonzero polynomial turns the interpolating function into a rigid-holomorphic one. It is used by [`CerednikDrinfeld.exists_holOn_affinoid_mul_pullback_eq_of_cover_clearing_of_cerednikDrinfeld_quotient`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.exists_holOn_affinoid_mul_pullback_eq_of_cover_clearing_of_cerednikDrinfeld_quotient), which assembles the edge regions into affinoids.
